  "live_records": {
    "q-36623": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36623",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $y=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}}{1+{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is not defined at the point $(0,0).$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
