    "q-72024": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72024",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $h(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(xy)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $h(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xy)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xy)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xy| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xy<1.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${h}_{y}$ is the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}} & ={D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
