    "q-132643": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132643",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
