    "q-132645": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132645",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y(x+z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & \\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132645-a",
              "type": "math",
